WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:06.260
Welcome to Grade 8 English, Unit
4: Advertising and Persuasion.

2
00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:11.760
Welcome to Unit 4 of Year 8: Advertising and Persuasion!

3
00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:17.500
Every day, you are surrounded by messages
designed to grab your attention, change your mind,

4
00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:19.480
or convince you to buy something.

5
00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:27.300
From billboards towering over busy Mogadishu streets to
sponsored posts sliding through your social media feed,

6
00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:29.220
advertising is everywhere.

7
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:30.800
But how does it work?

8
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.440
What techniques do advertisers use to persuade people?

9
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:41.960
And how can you, as a critical thinker, tell the difference
between honest information and clever manipulation?

10
00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:48.420
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

11
00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:56.580
By the end of this unit, you will be able to read and
understand passages about advertising techniques, persuasion,

12
00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.160
and ethical marketing, and discuss their ideas confidently.

13
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:07.840
you will also identify and use prepositions preceding
nouns correctly in phrases like on sale, in stock,

14
00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.740
for a limited time, at a discount, and with interest.

15
00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:17.120
you will also form and use passive
voice sentences in the present and past:

16
00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:23.500
"the product was launched by the company" and
"the advert is designed to attract young people.".

17
00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.380
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

18
00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:34.780
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: aptitude,
adversary, cunning, momentum, bonanza, concession.

19
00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:40.440
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

20
00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:44.220
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

21
00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:53.220
The reading sequence includes Advertising and Persuasion:
source text 1 and Advertising and Persuasion: source text 2.

22
00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:54.600
Read actively.

23
00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.780
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

24
00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.780
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

25
00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.800
The first language focus is Prepositions Preceding Nouns.

26
00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:17.260
What it means and why it matters Prepositions are words that
show the relationship between a noun and other parts of a

27
00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:17.660
sentence.

28
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:26.000
When we say "prepositions preceding nouns," we mean
prepositions that come before nouns to create common phrases.

29
00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:35.900
In advertising, these phrases appear constantly: items on
sale, products in stock, offers valid for a limited time.

30
00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:43.380
Learning these fixed phrases will make your English sound
natural and help you understand advertisements more easily.

31
00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:50.320
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

32
00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:55.960
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: Vocabulary Expert Interview.

33
00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:00.680
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

34
00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.840
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

35
00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.440
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

36
00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.240
You are ready to begin.

37
00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:16.640
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

38
00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.580
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

39
00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:25.820
Use AI English for hints and feedback, and
bring your questions to the live sessions.
